  "text": "Sol says: “Yes. The flour stayed dry, and she baked the bread that day.”\n\nVisit Rina at the bakery. Water is dripping through a cracked roof tile.\n\nThe seed is aboard. Steer through the channel to Grandma’s blue landing.\n\nTalk to Grandma about Mara’s work and when she could join the gathering.\n\nThe listener is unavailable right now. You can type your answer instead.\n\nGrandma adds what she has learned today to the end of her waiting story.\n\nPip waits on the bank. The seed stays aboard until someone takes it out.\n\nThe boy plays his flute while a passenger whistles along on Mara’s boat.\n\nThat section is held by a rope. Release its attachment before moving it.\n\nSol will share the draft. An unfinished ending is a valid contribution.\n\nPip reads at the gathering with Sol and Grandma. Mara is still at work.\n\n“One I wrote for Grandma’s garden. I keep bringing it to work with me.”\n\nThis flower is waiting for Grandma to finish and share her own account.\n\nMara has Grandma’s story. She will read it after the last boat returns.\n\nPip promised to ask Grandma about starting later. He has not asked yet.\n\nCan mean to possess something; also helps describe an action or a need.\n\nThe latest save could not be read. Your earlier progress has been kept.\n\nSol has arrived with his manuscript. Mara can now cross to the garden.\n\nA place beside the water where boats stop so people can get on or off.\n\nThe microphone stopped. You can review what was recorded or try again.\n\nWhat does ‘kept his promise’ mean here? What did Pip do that shows it?\n\nFirst Rina bakes with the dry flour; later she visits Sol with a loaf.\n\nThe torn edges line up. Choose where the tape will hold them together.\n\nThese are the words and picture you are trying. Pip is still with Sol.",
